R('ch13','scale_supersaturation','Mineral scale formation occurs when the saturation index (SI) of a mineral exceeds zero.', 'A positive saturation index indicates thermodynamic supersaturation. Precipitation rate and deposition location additionally depend on nucleation, residence time, inhibitors and transport; SI > 0 alone does not establish plugging.')
S('ch13','scale_mass_basis',r'The mixing of formation water and seawater creates.*?mixing fractions of formation water and seawater respectively\.',r'''Scale potential depends on the actual ion inventory, not a universal seawater fraction. With additive water volumes, ion concentrations $C$ in kg/m³ and molecular weights $M$ in kg/mol, a complete-precipitation upper bound is

$$C_{\mathrm{BaSO_4,max}}=M_{\mathrm{BaSO_4}}\min\!\left[\frac{C_{\mathrm{Ba}}(1-f)}{M_{\mathrm{Ba}}},\frac{C_{\mathrm{SO_4}}f}{M_{\mathrm{SO_4}}}\right].$$

The minimum must be taken on a molar basis. For the next example, equal available moles occur at $f=0.0609$ (6.09% seawater), giving about 399 mg/L potential BaSO₄; a 10–90% grid misses this peak. Solubility and precipitation kinetics reduce or redistribute the deposited amount.''')
