R('ch14','mw_units','and $MW$ is the gas molecular weight (kg/kmol).','and $MW$ is the molar mass in kg/mol. The result is J/kg; divide by 1,000 for kJ/kg or by $g$ for metres. Inserting $Z_1$ is a constant-property real-gas approximation, not an exact isentropic EOS calculation.')
S('ch14','schultz',r'For real gases at high pressures, the ideal gas relations.*?(?=### 14\.3\.5)',r'''For a real gas, an endpoint Schultz calculation uses an isentropic reference state $2s$ at the discharge pressure. Define specific volume in m³/kg, enthalpy in J/kg and pressure in Pa:

$$n_s=\frac{\ln(P_2/P_1)}{\ln(v_1/v_{2s})},\qquad n_v=\frac{\ln(P_2/P_1)}{\ln(v_1/v_2)}.$$

$$f_s=\frac{h_{2s}-h_1}{[n_s/(n_s-1)](P_2v_{2s}-P_1v_1)},\qquad H_p=f_s\frac{n_v}{n_v-1}(P_2v_2-P_1v_1).$$

The derivative properties $X=(T/v)(\partial v/\partial T)_P-1$ and $Y=-(P/v)(\partial v/\partial P)_T$ assist real-gas analysis, but $Y/(X+1)$ is not the Schultz head factor. Endpoint methods approximate the compression path; verify high-pressure or near-critical cases against a converged small-step calculation and report the selected NeqSim polytropic method.\cite{foundationCCPmethods}

''', sources=['https://ccp-centrifugal-compressor-performance.readthedocs.io/en/stable/references/performance_functions.html'])
